  "centerline": "# Centerline\n\n**What it does:** shrinks each inked shape down to a one-pixel \"skeleton\", then follows it to\nmake strokes. A thick letter becomes a single line down its middle, the way you'd write it\nwith a pen instead of filling it in.\n\n**Analog history:** *To be written with Nick* (single-line fonts for engraving, Hershey fonts,\ntechnical-pen lettering…).\n\n**What the sliders do physically:**\n- **Minimum length:** strokes shorter than this are dropped. Raise it if the plot is full of\n  tiny ticks from dust or noise.\n- **Simplify:** how much small wiggles are straightened. More simplify means fewer pen\n  segments, so faster and smoother plots, but less detail.\n\nNote: the line has the pen's width, not the shape's width. Thick shapes get thinner.\nUse a fill step (coming later) to cover areas.\n",
  "plot": "# Place on paper\n\n**What it does:** scales the drawing to fit the paper inside the margin, puts the strokes in\nan order that keeps pen-up travel short, and estimates how long the plotter will take.\n\n**What the settings do physically:**\n- **Margin:** blank paper around the drawing, in millimetres.\n- **Stroke order:** \"Nearest first\" makes the pen hop to the closest unfinished stroke, which\n  can cut plot time a lot. \"As traced\" is useful when the drawing order is part of the work.\n- **Allow reversing strokes:** lets the pen start a stroke from whichever end is closer.\n\n**Time estimate:** based on the machine profile's speeds, acceleration and pen up/down time.\nTreat it as a rough guide until the profile is calibrated against a real plotter.\n",
  "threshold": "# Threshold\n\n**What it does:** every pixel darker than the *level* becomes ink; everything else stays\npaper. The result is one layer: one pass of one pen.\n\n**Analog history:** <!-- Nick: line film / lith film, high-contrast copy work, photocopier\n\"darkness\" knob… --> *To be written with Nick.*\n\n**What the sliders do physically:**\n- **Level:** moves the line between \"ink\" and \"paper\". Lower = only the darkest areas get\n  drawn, so less ink and a shorter plot.\n- **Automatic (Otsu):** picks the level that best splits the image's tones into two groups.\n  Good starting point for scans and high-contrast photos.\n- **Invert:** draw the light areas instead (useful on dark paper with a light pen).\n",
